    slug: "agent-workflow-tooling-opportunity",
    title: "Agent Workflow Tooling: The Category That Has Not Been Named Yet",
    category: "Building",
    date: "2026-07-16",
    thumbnail: "scatter-converge",
    excerpt:
      "Agent workflow tooling is one of the fastest-rising micro-SaaS categories and almost nobody is building the industry-specific version of it.",
    readTime: "6 min read",
    body: [
      "Agent workflow tooling sits on every list of micro-SaaS categories currently rising, alongside vertical SaaS and private AI for regulated sectors. It is also the least well defined of them, which is usually a sign that the category is early rather than crowded.",
      "The general-purpose version of this is being built by well-funded companies. The industry-specific version is almost entirely unbuilt, and that is the part available to a domain expert.",
      "## What the category actually covers",
      "When software starts completing tasks rather than assisting with them, a new set of operational problems appears. Somebody has to decide which tasks an agent handles and which escalate. Somebody has to review what it did. Somebody has to explain a decision to a customer or an auditor. Somebody has to notice when it starts getting worse.",
      "None of that is model capability. All of it is workflow, and the teams getting real results from agents are consistently the ones keeping humans in the loop at the decision points rather than removing them.",
      "## Why the general version will not serve everyone",
      "A horizontal agent management platform has to be neutral about what the agent does, which means it cannot encode any judgement about when escalation is correct. It gives you the controls and leaves the policy to you.",
      "But the policy is the hard part, and the policy is industry-specific. When should a clinical documentation agent escalate to a human coder? When should a lease review agent stop and ask? Those questions have real answers that vary by domain, and they cannot be configured by someone who has not done the job.",
      "## The shape of the opportunity",
      "The product is not an agent. It is the operating layer around agents for one industry: the escalation rules, the review queue, the audit trail, the quality drift monitoring, and the reporting that satisfies whoever governs that industry.",
      "This is unusually well suited to a domain expert, because every part of it is a judgement question dressed as a technical one. The technical parts are commodity. The judgement about when a machine should stop and ask a person is not available anywhere.",
      "## Why now rather than later",
      "The buyers are appearing now. Organisations that deployed agents in the last eighteen months are currently discovering that they have no way to review, govern, or explain the output, and they are discovering it in the same order every time: enthusiasm, volume, an incident, then a scramble for oversight.",
      "Being in the market at the scramble stage is considerably better than being there at the enthusiasm stage, because the budget exists and the problem is felt rather than anticipated.",
      "## How to enter",
      "Start with the review queue, not the orchestration. The review queue is the piece every buyer needs immediately, it is simple to build, and it is where the industry-specific judgement lives. Orchestration is where the funded competitors are, and it is the least differentiated part.",
      "Then add the audit trail, then the drift monitoring, in that order. Each one is independently sellable, which means you can charge before the full product exists.",
      "See [audit trails are a feature](/blog/audit-trails-are-a-feature) for why the second step matters more than it looks.",
    ],
    pullquote:
      "The technical parts are commodity. The judgement about when a machine should stop and ask a person is not available anywhere.",
